WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.976
Northstar’s original synthetic quarter has net income 84 and D&A 40.

00:00:07.216 --> 00:00:16.069
Assume interest and tax expenses equal cash paid, there are no other noncash items, and operating working capital consumed 50 in cash.

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:22.240
Its simplified operating cash flow is 84 + 40 − 50 = 74.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.528
EBITDA is still 180.

00:00:25.218 --> 00:00:26.093
Why the gap?

00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:32.178
EBITDA excludes the interest and tax costs while this cash-flow example includes their payments.

00:00:32.418 --> 00:00:36.258
It also misses the 50 tied up in operating working capital.

00:00:36.498 --> 00:00:42.173
For example, customers may not yet have paid invoices, or cash may be tied up in inventory.

00:00:42.413 --> 00:00:49.581
Changes in receivables, inventory and payables can alter cash without altering EBITDA by the same amount.

00:00:50.271 --> 00:00:52.724
Now assume capital expenditures of 90.

00:00:52.964 --> 00:01:00.303
Operating cash flow less capital spending is 74 − 90 = −16 for this exercise.

00:01:00.543 --> 00:01:05.727
That calculation still is not the bank balance or a complete view of financing needs.

00:01:05.967 --> 00:01:17.252
The actual cash-flow statement separates operating, investing and financing movements; debt principal repayments are financing movements in this simplified U.S. example.

00:01:17.942 --> 00:01:23.169
Pause and explain aloud: how could positive EBITDA coexist with a cash shortage?

00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:27.505
Include at least one timing issue and one real cash obligation.

00:01:27.745 --> 00:01:32.801
Do not use this deliberately simplified bridge as a universal cash-flow formula.

00:01:33.491 --> 00:01:34.579
Now it's your turn.

00:01:34.819 --> 00:01:42.307
The manager says, “EBITDA is 180, so the 90 machine purchase is easily funded.” Choose your next step.

